# Error Handling and Validation Rules

## Error Handling Strategy

- Avoid try-catch blocks unless absolutely necessary
- Use early returns for validation
- Validate inputs at function boundaries
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Log errors appropriately without exposing sensitive data

## Input Validation

- Always validate user input from commands
- Check file types before operations
- Validate editor state before actions
- Sanitize file paths and URIs
- Handle edge cases gracefully

## Editor Validation

- Check if editor exists before operations
- Validate file language before specific operations
- Check if selection exists before text operations
- Validate document state before modifications
- Handle unsaved changes appropriately

## User Feedback

- Show warning messages for invalid operations
- Provide helpful error messages
- Use consistent message patterns
- Show success feedback when appropriate
- Guide users to correct actions

## Defensive Programming

- Assume external APIs can fail
- Handle null/undefined values gracefully
- Use optional chaining when appropriate
- Provide fallback behaviors
- Validate assumptions with assertions

## Error Recovery

- Attempt to recover from errors when possible
- Provide alternative actions on failure
- Clean up resources on error
- Maintain consistent state after errors
- Log errors for debugging

